1
00:00:00,350 --> 00:00:02,722
Hello, and thank you for watching.

2
00:00:02,722 --> 00:00:03,769
This is Unmask.

3
00:00:03,769 --> 00:00:06,839
The token launch where bots lose their edge.

4
00:00:06,839 --> 00:00:09,490
Bid blind, win fair, check everything.

5
00:00:10,390 --> 00:00:13,727
On most token launches, whoever is fastest wins.

6
00:00:13,727 --> 00:00:20,540
Bots snipe the supply, regular buyers get what is left, and nobody can check what really happened.

7
00:00:21,040 --> 00:00:23,672
Unmask is a sealed-bid launch on NEAR.

8
00:00:23,672 --> 00:00:26,028
You seal your bid in your browser.

9
00:00:26,028 --> 00:00:29,076
Nobody sees any bid until the window closes.

10
00:00:29,076 --> 00:00:34,133
Then an engine opens them all and finds one price that every winner pays.

11
00:00:34,133 --> 00:00:36,350
And anyone can check the result.

12
00:00:36,850 --> 00:00:39,795
Here are the same eleven bids on two launches.

13
00:00:39,795 --> 00:00:43,444
On a first-come launch, the bots take most of the supply.

14
00:00:43,444 --> 00:00:47,989
On a sealed launch, being first means nothing, so the bots win nothing.

15
00:00:47,989 --> 00:00:51,830
The cyan bar is an agent bidding inside rules its owner set.

16
00:00:52,330 --> 00:00:53,833
This is an open launch.

17
00:00:53,833 --> 00:00:58,994
There is a countdown, and all anyone can see is how many sealed bids there are.

18
00:00:58,994 --> 00:01:00,692
Not who bid, not how much.

19
00:01:00,692 --> 00:01:07,160
To bid, you pick a limit price and a size, and the browser seals it before it leaves your computer.

20
00:01:07,660 --> 00:01:10,360
You can also let an agent bid for you.

21
00:01:10,360 --> 00:01:11,640
You set the rules:

22
00:01:11,640 --> 00:01:14,979
a maximum price, a maximum size, and an expiry.

23
00:01:14,979 --> 00:01:20,309
The agent can only bid inside them, and the check runs again at settlement.

24
00:01:20,309 --> 00:01:25,710
In this recording I am using the demo wallet, so the rules show as unsigned.

25
00:01:26,210 --> 00:01:29,037
You can send your refund to another chain.

26
00:01:29,037 --> 00:01:32,605
Type a bad address, and it is caught before you seal.

27
00:01:32,605 --> 00:01:35,499
Type a good one, and the warning goes away.

28
00:01:35,499 --> 00:01:37,990
Tokens can only go to a NEAR account.

29
00:01:38,490 --> 00:01:41,077
You can also pay from another chain.

30
00:01:41,077 --> 00:01:43,880
This is a live price from NEAR Intents:

31
00:01:43,880 --> 00:01:48,479
about five dollars of USDC on Arbitrum, to deliver five on NEAR.

32
00:01:48,479 --> 00:01:49,629
It is a preview.

33
00:01:49,629 --> 00:01:53,150
There is no deposit address, and nothing is paid.

34
00:01:53,650 --> 00:01:56,092
Now a launch whose window has closed.

35
00:01:56,092 --> 00:02:00,183
No more bids are accepted, and only the creator can settle it.

36
00:02:00,183 --> 00:02:05,528
For this recording I skip the wallet sign-in and call the very same settle route.

37
00:02:05,528 --> 00:02:08,630
The engine opens the bids, and finds one price.

38
00:02:09,130 --> 00:02:10,940
Three cents for everyone.

39
00:02:10,940 --> 00:02:14,995
One million tokens sold, thirty thousand dollars raised.

40
00:02:14,995 --> 00:02:18,906
Whatever they offered, every winner paid exactly this.

41
00:02:18,906 --> 00:02:21,802
The snipers bid low and were priced out.

42
00:02:21,802 --> 00:02:24,916
Two bids were left out, each with a reason.

43
00:02:24,916 --> 00:02:27,306
And this refund goes to Arbitrum.

44
00:02:27,306 --> 00:02:28,392
That is a plan:

45
00:02:28,392 --> 00:02:29,840
nothing is sent yet.

46
00:02:30,340 --> 00:02:31,972
Now the part that matters.

47
00:02:31,972 --> 00:02:39,003
This page rebuilds the input from the public numbers, runs the rule again in your browser, and checks the money.

48
00:02:39,003 --> 00:02:40,070
Four checks pass.

49
00:02:40,070 --> 00:02:41,325
Two say not checked:

50
00:02:41,325 --> 00:02:45,782
whether the code is the published code, and whether hardware signed it.

51
00:02:45,782 --> 00:02:49,800
We cannot check those from here, so they do not count as passes.

52
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:53,828
Here is a sample launch where we pretend someone cheated.

53
00:02:53,828 --> 00:02:55,066
The checks catch it:

54
00:02:55,066 --> 00:02:57,170
the rule gives a different answer.

55
00:02:57,170 --> 00:03:01,874
On a sample, the enclave signature is simulated, but the arithmetic is real.

56
00:03:01,874 --> 00:03:07,320
The point is that the result is something you can test, not something you have to trust.

57
00:03:07,820 --> 00:03:16,680
There is a list of launches, a page to create your own and set the rules once, and a playground to turn the bots up and watch what happens on each side.

58
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:19,168
We want to be plain about this.

59
00:03:19,168 --> 00:03:24,620
The auction engine, the sealing in your browser, and the live price preview are real.

60
00:03:24,620 --> 00:03:28,532
The enclave and the payment checks are development stand-ins.

61
00:03:28,532 --> 00:03:31,290
Nothing is attested yet, and no funds move.

62
00:03:31,790 --> 00:03:36,280
Fairness comes from the rule and the proof, not from trusting us.

63
00:03:36,280 --> 00:03:40,149
And where we cannot prove something yet, Unmask says so.

64
00:03:40,149 --> 00:03:40,840
Thank you.

